          explanation: 'The Matsya Purana\'s self-description of Vishnu as the fish avatar. At the end of each cosmic cycle, the universe dissolves into the ocean — and Vishnu as Matsya preserves the seeds of creation. At the beginning of the next cycle, he creates anew. The fish is the perfect symbol: it lives in water (the dissolved state) and yet contains within it the potential for new life. The Matsya Purana teaches that destruction and creation are two aspects of the same divine process.',
          science: 'Cosmic cycles and stellar evolution: stars are born from the remnants of previous stars — the elements forged in one generation become the building blocks of the next. The universe recycles matter continuously. The Matsya Purana\'s "I preserve at dissolution and create at the beginning" maps onto this: every ending contains the seeds of the next beginning.',
          lifeLesson: 'The Matsya Purana teaches that endings are not final. When something in your life dissolves — a relationship, a project, a phase — something is preserved within that dissolution. The practice is to trust the process: the fish (Matsya) is always present, preserving what is essential even when everything appears lost.',
